# gpsim command script: drive the PIC12F675 footswitch and snapshot GPIO.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#   A register-level functional check of the PIC shell (bypass_mcu_pic12f675.c)
#   running inside gpsim. The pure debounce core and the output drivers are
#   already covered by the host/formal suite; the PIC SHELL -- the TMR0 tick, the
#   footswitch read on GP5, the debounce integrate/step wiring, and the GPIO
#   outputs -- has no simavr equivalent, so this drives the real built HEX in
#   gpsim and asserts the observable register state at five checkpoints. The
#   wrapper test/pic/run_gpsim_test.sh parses the snapshots and turns them into
#   PASS/FAIL.
#
# HOW IT IS RUN (see the wrapper / `make pic12f675-test-gpsim`)
#   gpsim -i -pp12f675 <variant>_simcal.hex -c test/pic/pic12f675_footswitch_toggle.stc
#   NOTE THE IMAGE: this lane runs the DERIVED *_simcal.hex, not the shipping
#   HEX. An uninjected image cannot run at all on this part -- the startup
#   `CALL 0x3FF` walks off the end of flash and the part watchdog-resets in a
#   loop with main() never reached. See `make pic12f675-simcal`.
#   The image is supplied on the gpsim command line, so this script is
#   variant-agnostic. gpsim names the pins gpio0..gpio5.
#
# PINS (per src/bypass_pins_pic12f675.h)
#   GP5 = footswitch (input + weak pull-up; high = released, low = pressed)
#   GP0 = status LED  (high = effect ENGAGED, low = BYPASS)
#   GP1/GP2 = the active variant's control output(s)
#   Only GPIO is snapshotted, once per checkpoint: this part has no LATx, so the
#   port IS the latch and a second read would return the same register.
#
# TIMING -- EVERY NUMBER HERE IS RE-DERIVED, NOT SCALED FROM THE 10F32x
#   FOSC = 4 MHz -> instruction clock FOSC/4 = 1 MHz -> 1000 cycles per 1 ms.
#   The tick is NOT 1 ms on this part: TMR0 runs unprescaled and rolls over every
#   256 instruction cycles, and the shell counts four rollovers, so
#       1 tick = 4 * 256 = 1024 cycles = 1.024 ms.
#   The debounce counts TICKS, not milliseconds, so every checkpoint below is
#   placed on a tick boundary -- N * 1024 -- and the arithmetic is written in
#   ticks. PRESSED_THRESH(8) ticks register a press; RELEASE_THRESH(25) ticks
#   re-arm.
#
#   Blocking actuation steals ticks from the polled loop (the relay/mute drivers
#   busy-wait), so checkpoints sit well clear of those windows. Measured in gpsim
#   on the real images, for the placement below:
#     - relay variant's power-on bypass pulse holds GP1 until cycle 13312
#       (13 ticks) -- INIT_BYPASS at 23040 is 10 ticks clear of it;
#     - a toggle's relay coil pulse runs ~12288 cycles (12 ticks) and the mute
#       variant's second stage lands ~6144 cycles (6 ticks) after the first, so
#       neither has settled at PRESS1_LOW -- which is why the wrapper asserts
#       only the LED BIT there, never the full output pattern.
#
# SCENARIO (two MOMENTARY presses; the effect latches between them)
#   tick   0..25  (cyc      0..25600)  : released -> BYPASS (power-on default)
#   tick  25..55  (cyc  25600..56320)  : press #1 (30 ticks) -> ENGAGED, then released
#   tick  55..130 (cyc  56320..133120) : released, effect stays ENGAGED (latching)
#   tick 130..160 (cyc 133120..163840) : press #2 (30 ticks) -> back to BYPASS
#
# CHECKPOINTS (the wrapper keys on these exact === labels ===)
#   INIT_BYPASS  @ 23040  (tick 22.5) : LED off, GP5 == 1 (released). Past the
#                         relay variant's 13-tick power-on pulse.
#   PRESS1_EARLY @ 31744  (tick 31)   : LED still OFF, GP5 == 0 (pressed).
#                         This is 6 ticks after the press edge (tick 25) and
#                         2 ticks BEFORE the earliest legitimate toggle, which is
#                         tick 25 + PRESSED_THRESH(8) = tick 33 = cycle 33792 --
#                         measured, and it lands on that cycle exactly. It pins
#                         that the TICK GATES the loop: the debounce demands 8
#                         SEPARATED samples, so a correct firmware is still
#                         mid-debounce here. A firmware whose tick stopped gating
#                         (a T0IF poll that never re-blocks) free-runs; its loop
#                         still spends a few hundred instruction cycles per
#                         sample, so it crosses the threshold within roughly one
#                         tick of the edge -- around cycle 26600, five ticks
#                         BEFORE this checkpoint -> LED already ON here -> FAIL.
#   PRESS1_LOW   @ 38400  (tick 37.5) : GP5 == 0 (still pressed), LED == 1 (the
#                         effect toggled ON the press, not on release)
#   ENGAGED      @ 76800  (tick 75)   : LED on and latched, GP5 == 1, and every
#                         variant's output pattern has settled (last settle is
#                         the relay's, at cycle 46080)
#   BYPASS_AGAIN @ 204800 (tick 200)  : toggled back off; press #2 toggles at
#                         tick 138 (cycle 141312) and the relay finishes its
#                         pulse by cycle ~152600

stimulus asynchronous_stimulus
  initial_state 1
  { 25600, 0,
    56320, 1,
    133120, 0,
    163840, 1 }
  name fsw
end

node n1
attach n1 fsw gpio5

break c 23040
run
echo ===INIT_BYPASS===
gpio

break c 31744
run
echo ===PRESS1_EARLY===
gpio

break c 38400
run
echo ===PRESS1_LOW===
gpio

break c 76800
run
echo ===ENGAGED===
gpio

break c 204800
run
echo ===BYPASS_AGAIN===
gpio

quit
